WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
A rat hears a tone that once came just before a mild shock.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.200
What does its brain do with that tone?

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.760
Two things happen at once.

4
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.120
The rat stops moving, and its blood pressure rises.

5
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.310
Neither one is the feeling of fear.

6
00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.230
But both can be measured, and that lets us trace them in the brain.

7
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.110
First, is the response learned?

8
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.350
One group of rats hears tones that end in a shock.

9
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:33.803
A second group gets the same tones and the same shocks, but kept apart in time.

10
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.877
Later, the paired rats freeze far more to the tone alone.

11
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:41.217
That difference is our estimate of what was learned.

12
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.300
Where is it learned?

13
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.200
The tone travels to the amygdala, a cluster of nuclei deep in the brain.

14
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.760
The lateral amygdala is the input stage.

15
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.920
The central amygdala sends the signal out.

16
00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:57.343
Three routes leave it.

17
00:00:57.923 --> 00:01:00.903
One runs to the periaqueductal gray, in the midbrain.

18
00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:02.823
It drives freezing.

19
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:06.503
A second runs to the lateral hypothalamus.

20
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:09.083
It drives the rise in blood pressure.

21
00:01:10.083 --> 00:01:15.703
A third, mostly indirect, reaches a hormone chain: hypothalamus, pituitary, adrenal.

22
00:01:16.403 --> 00:01:18.263
In people, its hormone is cortisol.

23
00:01:19.383 --> 00:01:20.903
Two of these routes are fast.

24
00:01:21.823 --> 00:01:25.023
Freezing and the pressure rise appear while the tone is still playing.

25
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:27.597
The hormone route is slow.

26
00:01:28.437 --> 00:01:32.297
The adrenal must make its hormone, so levels peak tens of minutes later.

27
00:01:33.237 --> 00:01:35.097
Feedback then turns further release down.

28
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.340
So here is the rule.

29
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.180
A learned threat cue reaches the amygdala, then leaves by separate routes.

30
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:48.503
In this rat task, damaging neurons in one target impaired mainly its own response.

31
00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:51.363
Damage to the amygdala can impair both.

32
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:56.987
This map is for learned responses to a cue outside the body, measured in rats.

33
00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.897
It does not say what the rat feels.

34
00:02:00.897 --> 00:02:02.517
Later chapters show where the map stops.

35
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.987
The amygdala is needed to learn the threat.

36
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:09.947
Separate routes carry out the response, two fast and one slow.
